    "The grammatical units of English consist of words, phrases, clauses, and sentences.",
    "The primary clausal elements are subject, verb, direct object, complement, and adverbial modifier.",
    "Word order is essential in English for determining syntactic grammatical relations.",
    "A declarative sentence typically follows a canonical subject, verb, and object structural pattern.",
    "Auxiliary verbs combine with main verbs to express grammatical aspect, mood, and voice.",
    "A subordinate clause can function as an adverbial, adjectival, or nominal clausal constituent.",
    "Subject-verb agreement requires the finite verb to agree with the head noun in person and number.",
    "Relative clauses provide essential or descriptive supplementary information about a referent noun phrase.",
    "Prepositional phrases serve as locational, temporal, or circumstantial clausal adjuncts.",
    "Passive voice constructions shift focus from the thematic agent to the clausal patient or theme.",
    "Coordinating conjunctions join independent syntactic clauses of equivalent grammatical rank.",
    "Modal verbs indicate necessity, possibility, permission, and epistemic speaker probability.",
    "The continuous grammatical aspect conveys ongoing duration and uncompleted clausal action.",
    "The perfect aspect links past events with current communicative relevance in discourse.",
    "Conditional clauses express hypothetical dependencies and counterfactual outcome scenarios.",
    "Determiners precede nouns to indicate definite referential specificity and grammatical quantification.",
    "Infinitive and gerund verbal clauses can serve as sentential grammatical subjects or objects.",
    "Adverbial clauses modify the main clause by articulating rationale, condition, time, or concession.",
    "Compound-complex sentences combine multiple independent clauses with at least one dependent clause.",
    "Nominalization transforms active verbal actions into abstract noun phrase clausal arguments."
